WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.130 --> 00:00:05.060
If you’ve watched my review of Ex Machina,
you’ll know what a big fan I am of this

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:09.750
superlative sci-fi film starring Alicia Vikander,
Oscar Isaac and Domhnall Gleeson.

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:14.440
Let’s take a look at 5 things you probably
didn’t know about Ex Machina!

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:20.800
One of the biggest challenges of Ex Machina
was designing the film’s android, Ava, so

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:24.380
that she didn’t just look like the robots
we’ve seen on screen before.

00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:29.069
Writer and director Alex Garland worked on
the design with British comic book artist

00:00:29.069 --> 00:00:33.090
Jock, who he’d previously worked with on
the sci-fi action movie Dredd.

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:38.430
The breakthrough in making Ava’s design
feel fresh was covering her form in mesh,

00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:42.760
which means we can see straight through her
to her skeleton structure and inner workings.

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:47.900
However, in certain lighting conditions, the
mesh catches the light and so her body takes

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:49.510
on a more human form.

00:00:49.510 --> 00:00:56.780
It took 6 weeks to film Ex Machina, but the
post-production process took 6 months.

00:00:56.780 --> 00:01:01.880
The visual effects team spent hours matching
their animation to the movements of actress

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:06.759
Alicia Vikander, who plays Ava, and they translated
as much as possible of Vikander’s on-set

00:01:06.759 --> 00:01:09.090
performance into their CG model.

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:14.149
And as visual effects supervisor Andrew Whitehurst
wanted his team to approach Ava’s design

00:01:14.149 --> 00:01:18.299
with fresh eyes, he banned them from looking
at pictures of robots!

00:01:18.299 --> 00:01:23.829
Instead, they took inspiration from sculptures
by Brâncuși, modernist Bauhaus sculptures,

00:01:23.829 --> 00:01:26.679
Formula One suspension, and high-end concept
bicycles!

00:01:26.679 --> 00:01:33.030
And, to make sure they got Ava’s movements
right, they also researched human anatomy.

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:38.070
Ex Machina is set in Alaska at the home-come-research
facility of Nathan, the billionaire genius

00:01:38.070 --> 00:01:39.170
who created Ava.

00:01:39.170 --> 00:01:45.109
But what appears in the movie to be one location
in Alaska, in reality is two different locations

00:01:45.109 --> 00:01:46.340
in Norway!

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:51.389
Many of the interior and exterior shots in
Ex Machina are from a private residence and

00:01:51.389 --> 00:01:56.249
the Juvet Landscape Hotel, both of which are
located in the Norwegian Fjords outside a

00:01:56.249 --> 00:01:57.490
town called Valldal.

00:01:57.490 --> 00:02:02.380
Both the house and hotel were designed by
Norwegian architects Jensen &amp; Skodvin.

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:07.380
When combined, the two buildings’ features,
including glass walls and spectacular views

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:12.090
over the Norwegian wilderness, gave Ex Machina’s
filmmakers a place that looked like the kind

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:15.750
of beautiful, private world in which the super-rich
would live.

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:18.880
Filming also took place at Pinewood Studios
in the UK.

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:25.160
A number of interior sets, including Ava’s
observation room, were built at Pinewood to

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:29.630
match the design features of the Norwegian
locations, and blue-screen was used to add

00:02:29.630 --> 00:02:33.140
in the landscape through the windows.

00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:36.560
If you’ve read Alex Garland’s novel The
Beach, you’ll know that he’s a fan of

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:40.500
Francis Ford Coppola’s critically acclaimed
war movie Apocalypse Now.

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:44.970
And Garland sees definite similarities between
Ex Machina’s reclusive billionaire boss

00:02:44.970 --> 00:02:50.850
Nathan, played by Oscar Isaac, and Apocalypse
Now’s rogue US Army Special Forces operative,

00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:53.860
Colonel Walter E. Kurtz, played by Marlon
Brando.

00:02:53.860 --> 00:02:59.790
For instance, like Kurtz, Nathan has isolated
himself geographically and psychologically.

00:02:59.790 --> 00:03:04.980
For Nathan this has meant holing himself up
in his super-remote location to work on his

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:06.170
latest project.

00:03:06.170 --> 00:03:09.980
and like Kurtz, Nathan sees himself as a sort
of god.

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:14.760
Plus, as Garland’s said, Nathan’s “spent
too much time up the river and has gone a

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:15.760
bit crazy.

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:19.160
At the point we meet him, he’s already tipped
over the edge.”

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:25.730
Both Alicia Vikander, who stars as Ava, and
Sonoya Mizuno, who plays Nathan’s mysterious

00:03:25.730 --> 00:03:29.180
silent assistant Kyoko, have backgrounds in
ballet.

00:03:29.180 --> 00:03:34.020
Vikander attended the Royal Swedish Ballet
School for nine years until she landed a part

00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:36.740
in a Swedish TV series in her mid-teens.

00:03:36.740 --> 00:03:41.420
And Mizuno trained with the Royal Ballet School
in London before going on to join the National

00:03:41.420 --> 00:03:43.810
Ballet of Ireland and Scottish Ballet.

00:03:43.810 --> 00:03:49.240
According to director Alex Garland, the many
years both actresses spent training as ballerinas

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:53.710
was invaluable to their performances in Ex
Machina as it meant they had such discipline

00:03:53.710 --> 00:03:57.880
and control of their bodies that they could
express a great deal about their characters

00:03:57.880 --> 00:03:59.080
with just slight movements.

00:03:59.080 --> 00:04:03.730
Well, there you have it, 5 things you probably
didn't know about Ex Machina!

00:04:03.730 --> 00:04:07.650
Now, tell me, who’s your favourite robot
character in the movies?

